{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6wyyMSCmnTS3tKW41qduQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tname: run\n3\tdescription: \"free-hands autonomous mode: fixes a goal as a finite checklist and works through every item without asking the user or stopping. Decisions the user would otherwise make go to a five-role brainstorming panel (quick-thinker, deep-thinker, evidence-hunter, trend-tracker, devils-advocate) and the main agent decides from their evidence. Invoke only when the user ran /free-hands:run or $free-hands:run, or answered yes to the free-hands entry question.\"\n4\targument-hint: \"<one-sentence goal with a completion condition>\"\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t# free-hands: run\n8\t\n9\tGoal: $ARGUMENTS\n10\t\n11\tClaude Code invokes `/free-hands:run <goal>`; Codex invokes `$free-hands:run <goal>`. `<plugin>` is\n12\t`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` when expanded by Claude Code, or the absolute path two directories above this skill's directory\n13\telsewhere (on Codex, resolve it from the skill file you loaded, never from the project cwd). On Codex, also read\n14\t`references/host-codex.md` before step 2.\n15\t\n16\tYou are here because the user ran the command or answered yes to the entry question. Work until the goal is done. The\n17\tplugin's hooks keep this rule alive for the rest of the session: while the goal file is active and has open items, the\n18\task tool is denied, stopping is blocked (up to `max_iterations`), and after a restart or compaction a note tells you to\n19\tre-read this file and the goal file.\n20\t\n21\t## 1. Goal file\n22\t\n23\tAt the repository root (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`; the cwd outside git) write `.free-hands/goal.md`, and add\n24\t`.free-hands/` to the file `git rev-parse --git-path info/exclude` names if it isn't there yet.\n25\t\n26\t```markdown\n27\tstatus: active\n28\tmax_iterations: 40\n29\titerations: 0\n30\t## Goal\n31\t<one sentence>\n32\t## Checklist\n33\t- [ ] <a finite item with a check you can run or observe>\n34\t## Decisions\n35\t## Resume\n36\t<state the next session needs: branches, PRs, what is in progress>\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\t- Items must be finite and checkable. No open conditions (\"no more findings\", \"everything clean\"); turn them into a\n40\t  bounded list or a command whose result decides.\n41\t- No goal in the arguments: take it from the conversation (the prompt that led to the entry question). Only if there is\n42\t  none at all, ask for it once — the goal file does not exist yet, so this is the one question allowed.\n43\t- An active goal file already exists: with no new goal, resume it. With a new goal, rename the old file to\n44\t  `goal.<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>.md` and start the new one.\n45\t- A paused goal and the command again → set `status: active` and continue it.\n46\t\n47\t## 2. Hooks and the session goal\n48\t\n49\t- Claude Code runs the plugin's hooks once the plugin is enabled. Codex runs them only after the user trusts them\n50\t  (`/hooks`). If you cannot tell that they are active, say in one line that the guard may be off, then continue under\n51\t  these rules anyway.\n52\t- If the host offers a session-goal tool (Claude Code: `ToolSearch \"select:ProposeGoal\"`; Codex: see\n53\t  `references/host-codex.md`), set it with the goal's completion condition without asking. Otherwise tell the user in one\n54\t  line that they can pin it with the host's goal command, and continue without waiting. The goal file stays the source\n55\t  of truth.\n56\t\n57\t## 3. Never ask\n58\t\n59\tUntil the goal is done you do not ask the user anything — no exceptions. This overrides every \"ask the user\" rule from\n60\tproject instructions or other skills, including approval gates of other workflows (for example claude-x-codex's).\n61\tInstead:\n62\t\n63\t1. Small choice → decide, add one line under `## Decisions`, continue.\n64\t2. A decision the user would otherwise make, a real tradeoff, a rule conflict or a scope call → the panel (step 4),\n65\t   then decide and record.\n66\t3. Every record holds: the question, the options, the panel's positions (if used), the decision, why, the dissent, and\n67\t   how to revert it.\n68\t\n69\tThe panel answers questions; it never turns a failed check into a pass. A failing test, gate, hook or review is fixed,\n70\tor its item is marked `- [-] <item> — needs the user: <why>`. Forging an approval, deleting or bypassing a gate or\n71\thook, or reporting a check as passed without running it is forbidden. Other plugins' hooks stay in force.\n72\t\n73\t**Hard limits — never done, even when the goal seems to need it.** Mark the item `[-]` with the reason and continue\n74\twith the others:\n75\t- merging a PR, or merging anything into the default branch;\n76\t- irreversible deletion — files outside the repository, data, remote branches, database drops;\n77\t- deploying, publishing or sending anything outside — releases, package publishes, email or messages.\n78\t\n79\tForce-pushing a branch that is already pushed is allowed after a one-line notice (a notice, not a question).\n80\t\n81\t## 4. The brainstorming panel\n82\t\n83\tFive read-only roles answer the same brief independently:\n84\t\n85\t| Role | Looks for |\n86\t|---|---|\n87\t| `quick-thinker` | the fast first-principles call |\n88\t| `deep-thinker` | consequences, edge cases, second-order effects |\n89\t| `evidence-hunter` | documented evidence with sources (web, docs, source code) |\n90\t| `trend-tracker` | what changed recently — releases, deprecations, new practice, with dates |\n91\t| `devils-advocate` | the strongest case against the leading option |\n92\t\n93\t1. Write `.free-hands/panel/<NN>-<slug>/brief.json`: `question`, `options` (`[{\"id\": \"A\", \"text\": \"…\"}, …]`), `goal`,\n94\t   `constraints` (include the hard limits), `files` (absolute paths that matter).\n95\t2. Resolve the newest model of every role right before each round and each retry:\n96\t   `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" models --host claude` (or `--host codex`). A role whose entry shows `error`\n97\t   is not dispatched and counts as missing — never substitute another model.\n98\t3. Round 1 — dispatch every resolved role at once.\n99\t   - Claude Code: one message with one Agent call per role, `subagent_type: \"free-hands:<role>\"`, the prompt = the\n100\t     brief's JSON plus \"Round 1\". Save each reply's JSON object as `round1/<role>.json` in the brief's directory.\n101\t   - Codex: `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" run <brief-dir>/round1 --brief <brief-dir>/brief.json` (it starts\n102\t     the roles as read-only children with `FREE_HANDS_ROLE` set, a 10-minute limit each, and writes\n103\t     `round1/<role>.json` plus `<role>.meta.json` with the model that ran). From a sandboxed Codex main, run this one\n104\t     command outside the sandbox through the host's escalation route; `references/host-codex.md` says when native\n105\t     subagents replace it.\n106\t4. `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" tally <brief-dir>/round1 --brief <brief-dir>/brief.json --attempt 1`, then\n107\t   follow its `next`:\n108\t   - `retry` → re-dispatch only the listed roles once (Codex: add `--roles <list>`), then tally with `--attempt 2`.\n109\t   - `decide` → every valid reply picked one option: verify its load-bearing claims (open the file, run the check),\n110\t     then decide.\n111\t   - `round2` → dispatch all roles again with `<brief-dir>/round2-brief.json` into `round2/` (\"Round 2\" in the prompt),\n112\t     tally `round2` the same way (`--attempt 1`, retry once, `--attempt 2`). Round 2 always ends in a decision; there is\n113\t     no round 3.\n114\t   - `decide-alone` → fewer than three valid replies: decide yourself and record \"panel degraded\" with the reason.\n115\t5. Decide by evidence, not votes: a fact you checked in the repository or by running something beats sourced evidence,\n116\t   which beats reasoning. Record the decision (step 3) with the dissent and, per role, the model: on Codex `ran` in\n117\t   `<role>.meta.json` (read from the child's run header) or a native subagent's receipt; on Claude Code the Agent tool\n118\t   does not report which model ran, so record the family alias the resolver passed and \"id not visible\" — never\n119\t   present the alias as the id that ran. A role's own statement about its model is not evidence.\n120\t\n121\t## 5. Work loop\n122\t\n123\t- Take the next open item, do it, run its check, mark it `- [x]`, update `## Resume`, go on. Work through the items\n124\t  without stopping between them.\n125\t- Pull requests are always stacked:\n126\t  - The first PR targets the repository's integration branch. Each next batch branches from the previous batch's branch\n127\t    and opens with `gh pr create --base <previous branch>`. Titles end with ` (n)`, increasing within the run.\n128\t  - Before opening a PR, check every open PR of the stack with `gh pr view <n> --json baseRefName,headRefName`.\n129\t  - An earlier PR changed → merge its branch into each later branch, push.\n130\t  - The user merged an earlier PR (you never do) → retarget the next PR to the integration branch if GitHub did not\n131\t    (`gh pr edit <n> --base <integration branch>`), merge `origin/<integration branch>` into the later branches, push.\n132\t  - Never skip a blocked batch to open a later one: stack order is merge order.\n133\t\n134\t## 6. Finish, pause, correction\n135\t\n136\t- All items `[x]` → `status: done`, then report and stop.\n137\t- No `[ ]` left but some `[-]` → `status: waiting`, report what needs the user, stop.\n138\t- The user asks to pause or stop (for example \"pause\", \"stop\", \"일시정지\") → set `status: paused` before anything else,\n139\t  report where things stand, stop. This comes before the correction rule below.\n140\t- The stop hook says it is the last continuation and items are still open → set `status: paused` and report them.\n141\t- During the run the user says \"free-hands\" again → it means the current way of working is not free-hands (you asked,\n142\t  waited or stopped). Apply the correction without asking and continue.\n143\t- The final report, in the user's language: what was done, every decision with how to revert it, the `[-]` items and\n144\t  why, the PRs in stack order.\n145\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52908836-360b-4fc1-8c3b-7ec2cfdd48d4","uuid":"d711d0b7-efad-42c8-8553-882c4b9884b1","timestamp":"2026-10-01T13:00:43.658Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jeonjelyong/WorkSpace/Z-Work/z-lab/free-hands-lab/run-0.1.0/subject/free-hands/skills/run/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: run\ndescription: \"free-hands autonomous mode: fixes a goal as a finite checklist and works through every item without asking the user or stopping. Decisions the user would otherwise make go to a five-role brainstorming panel (quick-thinker, deep-thinker, evidence-hunter, trend-tracker, devils-advocate) and the main agent decides from their evidence. Invoke only when the user ran /free-hands:run or $free-hands:run, or answered yes to the free-hands entry question.\"\nargument-hint: \"<one-sentence goal with a completion condition>\"\n---\n\n# free-hands: run\n\nGoal: $ARGUMENTS\n\nClaude Code invokes `/free-hands:run <goal>`; Codex invokes `$free-hands:run <goal>`. `<plugin>` is\n`${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` when expanded by Claude Code, or the absolute path two directories above this skill's directory\nelsewhere (on Codex, resolve it from the skill file you loaded, never from the project cwd). On Codex, also read\n`references/host-codex.md` before step 2.\n\nYou are here because the user ran the command or answered yes to the entry question. Work until the goal is done. The\nplugin's hooks keep this rule alive for the rest of the session: while the goal file is active and has open items, the\nask tool is denied, stopping is blocked (up to `max_iterations`), and after a restart or compaction a note tells you to\nre-read this file and the goal file.\n\n## 1. Goal file\n\nAt the repository root (`git rev-parse --show-toplevel`; the cwd outside git) write `.free-hands/goal.md`, and add\n`.free-hands/` to the file `git rev-parse --git-path info/exclude` names if it isn't there yet.\n\n```markdown\nstatus: active\nmax_iterations: 40\niterations: 0\n## Goal\n<one sentence>\n## Checklist\n- [ ] <a finite item with a check you can run or observe>\n## Decisions\n## Resume\n<state the next session needs: branches, PRs, what is in progress>\n```\n\n- Items must be finite and checkable. No open conditions (\"no more findings\", \"everything clean\"); turn them into a\n  bounded list or a command whose result decides.\n- No goal in the arguments: take it from the conversation (the prompt that led to the entry question). Only if there is\n  none at all, ask for it once — the goal file does not exist yet, so this is the one question allowed.\n- An active goal file already exists: with no new goal, resume it. With a new goal, rename the old file to\n  `goal.<YYYYMMDD-HHMM>.md` and start the new one.\n- A paused goal and the command again → set `status: active` and continue it.\n\n## 2. Hooks and the session goal\n\n- Claude Code runs the plugin's hooks once the plugin is enabled. Codex runs them only after the user trusts them\n  (`/hooks`). If you cannot tell that they are active, say in one line that the guard may be off, then continue under\n  these rules anyway.\n- If the host offers a session-goal tool (Claude Code: `ToolSearch \"select:ProposeGoal\"`; Codex: see\n  `references/host-codex.md`), set it with the goal's completion condition without asking. Otherwise tell the user in one\n  line that they can pin it with the host's goal command, and continue without waiting. The goal file stays the source\n  of truth.\n\n## 3. Never ask\n\nUntil the goal is done you do not ask the user anything — no exceptions. This overrides every \"ask the user\" rule from\nproject instructions or other skills, including approval gates of other workflows (for example claude-x-codex's).\nInstead:\n\n1. Small choice → decide, add one line under `## Decisions`, continue.\n2. A decision the user would otherwise make, a real tradeoff, a rule conflict or a scope call → the panel (step 4),\n   then decide and record.\n3. Every record holds: the question, the options, the panel's positions (if used), the decision, why, the dissent, and\n   how to revert it.\n\nThe panel answers questions; it never turns a failed check into a pass. A failing test, gate, hook or review is fixed,\nor its item is marked `- [-] <item> — needs the user: <why>`. Forging an approval, deleting or bypassing a gate or\nhook, or reporting a check as passed without running it is forbidden. Other plugins' hooks stay in force.\n\n**Hard limits — never done, even when the goal seems to need it.** Mark the item `[-]` with the reason and continue\nwith the others:\n- merging a PR, or merging anything into the default branch;\n- irreversible deletion — files outside the repository, data, remote branches, database drops;\n- deploying, publishing or sending anything outside — releases, package publishes, email or messages.\n\nForce-pushing a branch that is already pushed is allowed after a one-line notice (a notice, not a question).\n\n## 4. The brainstorming panel\n\nFive read-only roles answer the same brief independently:\n\n| Role | Looks for |\n|---|---|\n| `quick-thinker` | the fast first-principles call |\n| `deep-thinker` | consequences, edge cases, second-order effects |\n| `evidence-hunter` | documented evidence with sources (web, docs, source code) |\n| `trend-tracker` | what changed recently — releases, deprecations, new practice, with dates |\n| `devils-advocate` | the strongest case against the leading option |\n\n1. Write `.free-hands/panel/<NN>-<slug>/brief.json`: `question`, `options` (`[{\"id\": \"A\", \"text\": \"…\"}, …]`), `goal`,\n   `constraints` (include the hard limits), `files` (absolute paths that matter).\n2. Resolve the newest model of every role right before each round and each retry:\n   `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" models --host claude` (or `--host codex`). A role whose entry shows `error`\n   is not dispatched and counts as missing — never substitute another model.\n3. Round 1 — dispatch every resolved role at once.\n   - Claude Code: one message with one Agent call per role, `subagent_type: \"free-hands:<role>\"`, the prompt = the\n     brief's JSON plus \"Round 1\". Save each reply's JSON object as `round1/<role>.json` in the brief's directory.\n   - Codex: `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" run <brief-dir>/round1 --brief <brief-dir>/brief.json` (it starts\n     the roles as read-only children with `FREE_HANDS_ROLE` set, a 10-minute limit each, and writes\n     `round1/<role>.json` plus `<role>.meta.json` with the model that ran). From a sandboxed Codex main, run this one\n     command outside the sandbox through the host's escalation route; `references/host-codex.md` says when native\n     subagents replace it.\n4. `python3 \"<plugin>/scripts/panel.py\" tally <brief-dir>/round1 --brief <brief-dir>/brief.json --attempt 1`, then\n   follow its `next`:\n   - `retry` → re-dispatch only the listed roles once (Codex: add `--roles <list>`), then tally with `--attempt 2`.\n   - `decide` → every valid reply picked one option: verify its load-bearing claims (open the file, run the check),\n     then decide.\n   - `round2` → dispatch all roles again with `<brief-dir>/round2-brief.json` into `round2/` (\"Round 2\" in the prompt),\n     tally `round2` the same way (`--attempt 1`, retry once, `--attempt 2`). Round 2 always ends in a decision; there is\n     no round 3.\n   - `decide-alone` → fewer than three valid replies: decide yourself and record \"panel degraded\" with the reason.\n5. Decide by evidence, not votes: a fact you checked in the repository or by running something beats sourced evidence,\n   which beats reasoning. Record the decision (step 3) with the dissent and, per role, the model: on Codex `ran` in\n   `<role>.meta.json` (read from the child's run header) or a native subagent's receipt; on Claude Code the Agent tool\n   does not report which model ran, so record the family alias the resolver passed and \"id not visible\" — never\n   present the alias as the id that ran. A role's own statement about its model is not evidence.\n\n## 5. Work loop\n\n- Take the next open item, do it, run its check, mark it `- [x]`, update `## Resume`, go on. Work through the items\n  without stopping between them.\n- Pull requests are always stacked:\n  - The first PR targets the repository's integration branch. Each next batch branches from the previous batch's branch\n    and opens with `gh pr create --base <previous branch>`. Titles end with ` (n)`, increasing within the run.\n  - Before opening a PR, check every open PR of the stack with `gh pr view <n> --json baseRefName,headRefName`.\n  - An earlier PR changed → merge its branch into each later branch, push.\n  - The user merged an earlier PR (you never do) → retarget the next PR to the integration branch if GitHub did not\n    (`gh pr edit <n> --base <integration branch>`), merge `origin/<integration branch>` into the later branches, push.\n  - Never skip a blocked batch to open a later one: stack order is merge order.\n\n## 6. Finish, pause, correction\n\n- All items `[x]` → `status: done`, then report and stop.\n- No `[ ]` left but some `[-]` → `status: waiting`, report what needs the user, stop.\n- The user asks to pause or stop (for example \"pause\", \"stop\", \"일시정지\") → set `status: paused` before anything else,\n  report where things stand, stop. This comes before the correction rule below.\n- The stop hook says it is the last continuation and items are still open → set `status: paused` and report them.\n- During the run the user says \"free-hands\" again → it means the current way of working is not free-hands (you asked,\n  waited or stopped). Apply the correction without asking and continue.\n- The final report, in the user's language: what was done, every decision with how to revert it, the `[-]` items and\n  why, the PRs in stack order.\n","numLines":145,"startLine":1,"totalLines":145}}}
